according to sean serrania the rockets  and the thunder  are planning to boycott game five of  their series as well so  as promised a developing situation let's  head back down to orlando  hard working rebecca harlow what's the  latest you're hearing down there  yes well chris buck's general manager  john horst was very respectful in  addressing  the media now just so you know there  were only about a handful of us  back outside of the bucs locker room  waiting for any kind of word from the  team he let us know that there will be  an official statement  coming from the bucks organization but  that likely the players would remain  inside of the locker room for a while so  he told us  that we didn't necessarily need to wait  the players might not speak today they  might not even speak tomorrow  but again that there would be an  official statement coming from the bucks  but what i can tell you guys is this  being around here in the bubble i have  talked to  several bucks players who have told me  that as a team their initiative  is all about creating awareness around  voting  wesley matthews has been very vocal in  that he wears  vote on the back of his jersey some of  the things that they're looking to do at  fizzer  forum back at their home arena they want  to make that a voting site so there are  different initiatives that they really  want to do to create that real systemic  change but again an official statement  might not be coming today  now i've texted with several players  from other teams who tell me  they're also watching this unfolding and  that they're undecided  as to whether or not they'll be  boycotting their games as well  moving forward those players that you've  uh texted and spoken to have they said  that there's anything that they want in  particular as an  end result and do any of them have plans  on leaving orlando because that's a very  different situation  no i haven't heard anything like that  i've not been getting  the sense or the message from any  players that they're planning on leaving  orlando now that doesn't mean that they  might not make that decision  or make the decision to stay but right  now everything just feels very up in the  air but what i can  say is that the players feel very  strongly  about creating that systemic change it's  not just about wearing a t-shirt it's  not just about  you know pictures or shoes it's about  how to create  real change for americans so  that is the message that they're  continuing to try to get out here in  orlando  rebecca harlow thank you for your report  yet again  we appreciate you let's  uh head now to stephanie reddy who is  also  was scheduled to be on the sidelines for  tonight's okc  houston game according to multiple  reports  that game also planning to be boycotted  as well  stephanie can you confirm those reports  what is the latest  i have not hello chris i've not been  given any official word but i can just  tell you what i have witnessed because  as you mentioned i  am here in the building um at about 4 15  there were players on the court  shooting getting shots up warming up  coaches sitting on the bench as you know  is often uh the case when the coaches  are waiting for players to come out and  then this happened we see someone  leaving  the locker room from the thunder and we  can follow them you can see now the  gentleman in the gray shirt he comes  out and he appears to deliver a message  because you can see all the heads turn  and the blue jerseys the blue shirts  players and assistant coaches that are  helping those players get warmed up and  then they all  leave the court to go to the back nearly  around that same time  i also witnessed houston rockets  officials the staff  some of the coaches that were waiting  for players to come out they also went  into the back  of the um arena area where their locker  room is  now that was 4 15 and now here we are 30  minutes later and things have escalated  and progressed quite quickly shortly  after that we're going to show you some  film  where we saw russell westbrook and chris  paul  they were meeting in a room together  here they are leaving it was about 10  minutes  they were in that room now the reason  why this is important is not just  because they're leaders for their two  teams  but as you may know chris paul is the  president of  the nbpa so anything that happens as it  relates to the players union  he obviously is going to have a lot of  say in what's going on  shortly thereafter we did see  players leaving the building so as i've  mentioned i have not been  told anything official as it relates to  this game that was supposed to start at  6 30  but we will be showing you pictures  players leaving the building  now i have been texting with coaches and  players from other teams to see what  they know  and i've been told that they're united  they want change  i texted with someone with the orlando  magic because i was very curious  is this a decision the bucks made  unilaterally unilaterally excuse me  or was this a joint decision they made  with the orlando magic because we saw  both teams leaving the building going to  their buses i was told  absolutely they are all united they want  change that has been the united message  now remember when this all started the  talk of boycotting it was with the  boston celtics and the toronto raptors  they're supposed to play tomorrow they  were the first two teams that actually  broached this topic publicly  they already had a meeting together i  was told they are still planning on  meeting tonight  the two teams boston and toronto because  they still did not come to a final  decision  about what they wanted to do with their  game things have obviously changed quite  quickly  because now we have two games  potentially we have not received  official word about this one yet  but two games in the nba that are being  boycotted by the players  to make a united stand for the things  that they believe in the reason why they  said they were here not just to try to  win a championship but to also use their  platform  use their voices to initiate change so  chris it is very fluid here i'll do my  best to keep you up to date  stephanie first and foremost great job  you answered about five  follow-up questions with your reporting  so thank you for that but i just want  one clarification when you say the  players say that they  want change we understand that's not  with  their situation in orlando that's with  social justice is there a particular  thing  that they've said that they want when  they say we want change  you know i've i've heard many things  that they're looking to change  obviously you hear people talking a lot  about voting and how important that is  because that  is an exact change that you can make if  you can affect  policy then you can affect the change so  you hear players talking about voting  registration making sure they get as  many people out to the ballot box as  possible  but you also hear the talk about police  brutality and i think this latest  incident in kenosha wisconsin has  sparked that  again they say that it's unfortunate  they're using their voices they're  wearing the shirts it's printed on the  court that black lives matter  they don't feel like it's enough they  don't feel like  their voices are being heard strong  enough so this  is one more way that the players of the  national basketball association  are trying to affect change
 

